WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.000
Hello everyone, we are at DEFCON at the Creator Corner.

00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:07.520
First of all, thanks for TikTok for sponsoring the Creator Corner

00:00:07.520 --> 00:00:09.280
and to do the background interviews.

00:00:09.280 --> 00:00:11.360
Thanks for Hackerone and also for helping organize it

00:00:11.360 --> 00:00:13.360
and all the other sponsors as well.

00:00:13.360 --> 00:00:15.120
I have amazing guests today.

00:00:15.120 --> 00:00:16.719
We have Jason Haddix.

00:00:16.719 --> 00:00:17.520
Hello.

00:00:17.520 --> 00:00:18.799
And we have Blaklis.

00:00:18.799 --> 00:00:19.520
Yeah, hello.

00:00:19.520 --> 00:00:20.719
Nice to meet you here.

00:00:20.719 --> 00:00:23.040
Can you start with, you can just say,

00:00:23.040 --> 00:00:24.959
where are you at your current journey?

00:00:24.959 --> 00:00:27.120
Are you focusing on the background team or something else?

00:00:27.120 --> 00:00:27.840
Where are you at?

00:00:27.840 --> 00:00:29.440
Jason, can you start with you, please?

00:00:29.440 --> 00:00:30.000
Yeah, absolutely.

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:31.760
So I do a little bit of everything right now.

00:00:31.760 --> 00:00:33.599
So I just started my own company,

00:00:33.599 --> 00:00:37.200
which is a red teaming and penetration testing company

00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:39.680
after working for other people my whole life.

00:00:41.200 --> 00:00:44.160
But it is heavily influenced by my bug bounty journey.

00:00:44.160 --> 00:00:47.919
So a lot of techniques and stuff that are more modern

00:00:47.919 --> 00:00:50.160
that you see with bug bounty hunters and stuff like that.

00:00:50.160 --> 00:00:52.720
So I still do bug bounty hunting on the side

00:00:52.720 --> 00:00:56.000
and my company does training as well for bug bounty hunters,

00:00:56.000 --> 00:00:57.919
which we did a talk here at DEFCON,

00:00:57.919 --> 00:01:00.240
which I released a couple of methods and stuff like that

00:01:00.959 --> 00:01:01.599
from the training.

00:01:01.599 --> 00:01:02.560
Awesome.

00:01:02.560 --> 00:01:03.200
Thank you very much.

00:01:03.200 --> 00:01:03.759
Thank you, yeah.

00:01:03.759 --> 00:01:07.279
So really, I'm just trying to get my company off the ground

00:01:07.279 --> 00:01:10.320
and, you know, in the interim, find some bugs,

00:01:10.320 --> 00:01:11.919
make sure that you stay liquid.

00:01:12.800 --> 00:01:13.839
Yeah, so.

00:01:13.839 --> 00:01:15.680
Do you also do some, like, group hunting?

00:01:16.720 --> 00:01:17.279
Do, yeah.

00:01:17.919 --> 00:01:20.480
So people who come to the course,

00:01:20.480 --> 00:01:22.720
I think we've had about, at this point,

00:01:22.720 --> 00:01:25.360
maybe 250 or 300 people come to the course.

00:01:25.360 --> 00:01:27.519
We have a private Discord that they get access to

00:01:27.680 --> 00:01:28.639
and we hunt together on a program.

00:01:28.639 --> 00:01:31.760
So every two months, we choose usually a wide scope program.

00:01:32.639 --> 00:01:36.879
So we started with FIS, then we moved to T-Mobile,

00:01:36.879 --> 00:01:38.400
and then we moved to BMW.

00:01:39.519 --> 00:01:40.720
So yeah, we've been hunting on those,

00:01:40.720 --> 00:01:41.919
having really great success.

00:01:41.919 --> 00:01:43.440
Yeah, I feel like this also gives you

00:01:43.440 --> 00:01:46.080
a really nice perspective into, like,

00:01:46.080 --> 00:01:48.080
first, you see a lot of different people hunting,

00:01:48.080 --> 00:01:49.599
you know what mistakes they are making.

00:01:49.599 --> 00:01:51.199
I hope to really pick your mind

00:01:51.199 --> 00:01:52.480
on some of the lessons that you've had.

00:01:52.480 --> 00:01:53.599
Yeah, absolutely, yeah.

00:01:53.599 --> 00:01:54.000
Yeah.

00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:54.559
How about you?

00:01:54.559 --> 00:01:57.440
I know that you had amazing time at Ambassador Awards,

00:01:58.400 --> 00:02:00.639
being so much ahead of everyone.

00:02:00.639 --> 00:02:02.879
Absolutely, I'm pretty proud of that.

00:02:02.879 --> 00:02:03.360
Yeah.

00:02:03.360 --> 00:02:07.120
Yeah, I have quite the inverse situation of you.

00:02:07.760 --> 00:02:10.960
I co-created a company a few years ago

00:02:10.960 --> 00:02:13.119
and I decided to stop everything

00:02:13.119 --> 00:02:15.279
and get back to BugBunty full-time

00:02:15.279 --> 00:02:18.559
because, like, I really value the freedom

00:02:19.119 --> 00:02:21.039
that you can get with BugBunty.

00:02:21.039 --> 00:02:24.399
So I decided to, like, keep my company

00:02:24.399 --> 00:02:26.960
and do BugBunty full-time again.

00:02:27.520 --> 00:02:30.639
And yeah, I'm trying to help the community a lot.

00:02:31.360 --> 00:02:34.240
So I'm very active on communities,

00:02:34.240 --> 00:02:36.720
on Discord and on Twitter also.

00:02:36.720 --> 00:02:39.279
So I'm trying to answer every questions

00:02:39.279 --> 00:02:40.479
and things like that.

00:02:40.479 --> 00:02:42.720
And I'm super active also

00:02:42.720 --> 00:02:44.639
in the French community specifically.

00:02:45.199 --> 00:02:47.839
We have, like, a quite large community

00:02:48.399 --> 00:02:51.279
with maybe something like 700 people.

00:02:51.279 --> 00:02:52.800
And I'm also managing

00:02:53.440 --> 00:02:58.240
the Ambassador Awards with a little club.

00:02:58.240 --> 00:03:02.639
And yeah, we did pretty good at the World Cup.

00:03:02.639 --> 00:03:03.600
So yeah, pretty good.

00:03:03.600 --> 00:03:05.199
How did the line-up of events go for you?

00:03:06.479 --> 00:03:07.279
Pretty good.

00:03:07.279 --> 00:03:09.360
For me personally, that was pretty good.

00:03:09.360 --> 00:03:10.880
And it was super fun also

00:03:10.880 --> 00:03:13.520
because there's a lot of French people.

00:03:13.520 --> 00:03:14.800
For the first time, I guess,

00:03:14.800 --> 00:03:17.520
we were, like, 700 from France.

00:03:17.520 --> 00:03:20.320
So that's definitely, like, a special event.

00:03:20.320 --> 00:03:22.720
But yeah, personally, it was pretty good.

00:03:23.119 --> 00:03:24.880
I focused on the Epic Games.

00:03:24.880 --> 00:03:26.559
And that was super good.

00:03:26.559 --> 00:03:27.279
Awesome.

00:03:27.279 --> 00:03:28.479
Okay, we have the first question

00:03:28.479 --> 00:03:30.080
from Simone from Twitter.

00:03:30.080 --> 00:03:32.639
Will bug bounty hunting remain a viable option

00:03:32.639 --> 00:03:34.240
as a full-time career?

00:03:34.240 --> 00:03:37.279
And is there an equation that tells us

00:03:37.279 --> 00:03:39.600
where is the moment to quit and go full-time?

00:03:39.600 --> 00:03:40.720
Jason, let me start with you.

00:03:42.000 --> 00:03:43.679
Yeah, I don't think bug bounty

00:03:43.679 --> 00:03:44.720
is going anywhere, honestly.

00:03:44.720 --> 00:03:50.559
I think that it only grows into more things

00:03:50.559 --> 00:03:52.240
that it currently isn't into right now.

00:03:53.199 --> 00:03:54.639
At least from the conversations I've had

00:03:54.639 --> 00:03:55.839
with a lot of the platforms,

00:03:56.720 --> 00:03:57.679
they want to get to the point

00:03:57.679 --> 00:04:00.800
where you do things other than just hack things, right?

00:04:00.800 --> 00:04:02.080
And hack different types of things.

00:04:02.080 --> 00:04:03.600
They want to do defensive stuff, right?

00:04:05.279 --> 00:04:07.119
Log ingestion templates,

00:04:07.119 --> 00:04:08.960
queries, alert templates.

00:04:10.160 --> 00:04:11.199
They want to get to the point

00:04:11.199 --> 00:04:13.440
where they can host code.

00:04:13.440 --> 00:04:14.479
You can do code review.

00:04:15.039 --> 00:04:16.959
So I think that the gig economy

00:04:16.959 --> 00:04:19.119
for security people grows even more

00:04:19.119 --> 00:04:20.880
over the next five or six years,

00:04:20.880 --> 00:04:22.399
which brings in new types of people,

00:04:22.399 --> 00:04:23.920
which I'm kind of excited about, right?

00:04:23.920 --> 00:04:25.920
That's not stuff I do every day.

00:04:25.920 --> 00:04:27.440
So it'd be cool to check that out.

00:04:28.640 --> 00:04:30.959
And so I think that when that expands,

00:04:31.760 --> 00:04:35.200
you have other people who come in

00:04:35.200 --> 00:04:36.880
and then you have the platforms

00:04:36.880 --> 00:04:37.920
that keep on pushing

00:04:37.920 --> 00:04:39.679
and then you have more opportunity.

00:04:39.679 --> 00:04:41.200
So I think that going full-time

00:04:42.000 --> 00:04:44.640
can become a viable option for a lot of people.

00:04:45.600 --> 00:04:48.000
I also know, though, from my conversations

00:04:48.000 --> 00:04:50.239
with bug hunters who take my class,

00:04:50.320 --> 00:04:52.399
then all the private discords

00:04:52.399 --> 00:04:53.600
that exist and stuff like that,

00:04:54.239 --> 00:04:57.279
there is a little bit of a backlash right now

00:04:57.279 --> 00:05:00.799
of maybe the talent in the industry

00:05:00.799 --> 00:05:02.480
is not being treated as well as they used to be

00:05:02.480 --> 00:05:03.600
by some of the platforms.

00:05:03.600 --> 00:05:05.119
And so a lot of people

00:05:05.119 --> 00:05:07.279
are kind of moving to pentest models,

00:05:07.839 --> 00:05:09.839
but hosted by companies

00:05:09.839 --> 00:05:11.920
who do hybrid bug bounty kind of models, right?

00:05:11.920 --> 00:05:13.519
So they're pentest companies,

00:05:13.519 --> 00:05:16.079
but you go 1099 work for them like bug bounty.

00:05:16.079 --> 00:05:18.720
And so you can get guaranteed money for your time.

00:05:18.720 --> 00:05:20.239
It may not be a ton,

00:05:20.239 --> 00:05:22.399
but it is enough to sweep by.

00:05:22.399 --> 00:05:23.679
So it'd be interesting to see

00:05:23.679 --> 00:05:28.480
how the talent wants to evolve their journeys.

00:05:28.480 --> 00:05:29.200
Yeah.

00:05:29.200 --> 00:05:31.679
Yeah, you said you quit your company

00:05:31.679 --> 00:05:33.200
for a full-time bug bounty.

00:05:33.200 --> 00:05:36.239
Did you look for a specific equation

00:05:36.239 --> 00:05:37.679
as the question set of like,

00:05:37.679 --> 00:05:39.760
okay, this many bugs,

00:05:39.760 --> 00:05:40.959
this many agents, whatever,

00:05:40.959 --> 00:05:41.920
now I can quit?

00:05:41.920 --> 00:05:42.880
Or for you it's just...

00:05:43.600 --> 00:05:47.040
I mean, I was doing bug bounty for a long time.

00:05:47.040 --> 00:05:48.799
It was like a side of my job.

00:05:50.480 --> 00:05:53.200
I mean, I knew that it was already profitable.

00:05:53.200 --> 00:05:54.720
And I think that's quite important

00:05:54.720 --> 00:05:56.399
because when you're doing bug bounty full-time,

00:05:56.399 --> 00:06:01.519
you're like relying on like your own rentability

00:06:01.519 --> 00:06:04.480
and you need to have some success.

00:06:04.480 --> 00:06:05.440
That's quite important

00:06:05.440 --> 00:06:09.119
because if you are not sure about that,

00:06:09.119 --> 00:06:11.200
that would be a super stressful job

00:06:11.200 --> 00:06:12.399
doing bug bounty full-time

00:06:12.399 --> 00:06:16.640
with like you're not sure of getting enough money

00:06:17.200 --> 00:06:18.079
or something like that.

00:06:18.079 --> 00:06:20.239
That can be quite stressful.

00:06:20.239 --> 00:06:23.040
And definitely you won't like the job

00:06:23.040 --> 00:06:24.640
of doing bug bounty full-time.

00:06:24.640 --> 00:06:27.359
If you don't have like a bit of savings,

00:06:27.359 --> 00:06:29.600
maybe one or two years of savings

00:06:29.600 --> 00:06:30.799
or something like that,

00:06:30.799 --> 00:06:33.519
that's definitely maybe not a good idea

00:06:34.399 --> 00:06:37.359
to get to full-time bug bounty.

00:06:37.359 --> 00:06:40.320
So yeah, I'd say one thing that is important

00:06:40.320 --> 00:06:42.079
is having some savings.

00:06:42.079 --> 00:06:45.119
And I've tried maybe for a year or something

00:06:45.119 --> 00:06:46.559
and see if you're like,

00:06:46.559 --> 00:06:49.359
you're getting enough money to just come there.

00:06:50.640 --> 00:06:52.480
I'd say that's pretty much important.

00:06:52.480 --> 00:06:53.519
Yeah, of course.

00:06:54.320 --> 00:06:55.839
Another question from Nate.

00:06:57.279 --> 00:06:58.399
It's actually very relevant

00:06:58.399 --> 00:07:01.359
because I created the content here yesterday

00:07:01.359 --> 00:07:04.720
with Mattel who's 16 years old and has crazy bugs.

00:07:04.720 --> 00:07:05.600
And the question says,

00:07:06.160 --> 00:07:07.679
many successful bug bounty hunters

00:07:07.679 --> 00:07:10.799
started their journey into hacking at a very young age.

00:07:11.359 --> 00:07:13.519
Do you think natural talent is essential

00:07:13.519 --> 00:07:15.040
for success in bug bounty

00:07:15.040 --> 00:07:17.279
or hard work and determination can be enough?

00:07:18.880 --> 00:07:21.440
I think hard work and determination can be enough.

00:07:21.440 --> 00:07:22.480
I've seen plenty of people

00:07:22.480 --> 00:07:25.359
who are older crush bug bounty.

00:07:25.359 --> 00:07:27.279
In fact, some of the people

00:07:27.279 --> 00:07:29.440
at the million-dollar hacker market are very old.

00:07:31.679 --> 00:07:33.839
And then there's more of those million-dollar hacker people.

00:07:34.399 --> 00:07:37.119
If you were to count all the platform work

00:07:37.119 --> 00:07:38.959
that they do on all the different platforms,

00:07:38.959 --> 00:07:40.079
like the million-dollar hacker work

00:07:40.079 --> 00:07:41.600
is usually a hacker one thing.

00:07:41.839 --> 00:07:42.339
Yeah.

00:07:42.799 --> 00:07:44.880
But many people have crossed the million-dollar mark

00:07:45.600 --> 00:07:46.720
across all platforms.

00:07:46.720 --> 00:07:49.519
And they're in there.

00:07:49.519 --> 00:07:51.440
Mark Wichfield is a good example.

00:07:51.440 --> 00:07:54.720
He's one of the best at SQL injection in the world

00:07:54.720 --> 00:07:57.440
and account takeover bugs and access bugs that I know.

00:07:57.440 --> 00:07:59.519
I've met, I've talked to him, I've interviewed him.

00:08:01.600 --> 00:08:04.559
And I think he's upwards of 50 or something like that.

00:08:04.559 --> 00:08:06.720
So I think it's hard work and determination.

00:08:06.720 --> 00:08:09.679
I think it's also one that's not talked about in that

00:08:09.679 --> 00:08:13.279
is that also you could start late

00:08:13.279 --> 00:08:15.119
and still be successful if you grind.

00:08:15.119 --> 00:08:18.720
But also a lot of us have had 20 plus years of pen test

00:08:18.720 --> 00:08:20.239
or security work under our belt

00:08:20.239 --> 00:08:21.760
and then transition into bug bounty.

00:08:21.760 --> 00:08:25.200
So there's a little bit of longevity

00:08:25.200 --> 00:08:29.040
and exposure to the world that helps as well.

00:08:29.760 --> 00:08:30.559
A lot of people think,

00:08:30.559 --> 00:08:32.799
oh, I'm just going to go into bug bounty

00:08:32.799 --> 00:08:34.880
without ever having had any pen test experience

00:08:34.880 --> 00:08:35.599
or anything.

00:08:36.239 --> 00:08:38.159
That's a hard transition

00:08:38.159 --> 00:08:39.760
to move into offensive security

00:08:39.760 --> 00:08:41.280
purely just to go into bug bounty.

00:08:41.280 --> 00:08:42.000
So yeah.

00:08:42.000 --> 00:08:42.500
Yeah.

00:08:43.359 --> 00:08:43.919
Yeah.

00:08:43.919 --> 00:08:46.559
Maybe we won't answer each question for both of you.

00:08:47.440 --> 00:08:48.960
I'll ask another question to you.

00:08:48.960 --> 00:08:50.640
It's also very relevant to this time

00:08:50.640 --> 00:08:52.799
because we had so much good research now.

00:08:53.520 --> 00:08:54.960
And the question says,

00:08:54.960 --> 00:08:57.919
generally researchers post content of their research

00:08:57.919 --> 00:08:59.840
after mass reporting the vulnerability

00:08:59.840 --> 00:09:00.960
in bug bounty programs.

00:09:01.520 --> 00:09:03.200
It sometimes can be discouraged

00:09:03.200 --> 00:09:05.200
of getting duplicates on these bugs.

00:09:05.200 --> 00:09:06.719
What is your take on this?

00:09:07.359 --> 00:09:08.640
What will you do with the research

00:09:08.640 --> 00:09:10.159
that you're so inexperienced?

00:09:11.760 --> 00:09:12.559
I agree with that.

00:09:13.760 --> 00:09:16.479
I'm definitely posting my research

00:09:16.479 --> 00:09:18.239
once I was paid for that

00:09:18.239 --> 00:09:21.039
because if I spend a lot of time

00:09:21.039 --> 00:09:23.679
researching from something that is quite new,

00:09:23.679 --> 00:09:25.119
I'm definitely using that.

00:09:25.679 --> 00:09:28.559
I need to be paid for my research,

00:09:28.559 --> 00:09:31.599
but that doesn't make it not valuable

00:09:31.599 --> 00:09:34.960
because there's a lot of people

00:09:34.960 --> 00:09:36.799
that can be impacted by my research

00:09:36.799 --> 00:09:38.719
and I will probably focus on

00:09:38.719 --> 00:09:41.840
maybe five, six, seven, eight programs.

00:09:41.840 --> 00:09:43.760
That doesn't mean that there won't be

00:09:45.039 --> 00:09:48.239
anything vulnerable again after that.

00:09:48.239 --> 00:09:50.960
But eventually you will need maybe

00:09:50.960 --> 00:09:53.760
to do some recon and something like that

00:09:53.760 --> 00:09:56.719
to find the targets I missed

00:09:56.719 --> 00:10:00.400
or the targets every paper will miss.

00:10:00.400 --> 00:10:03.119
That's pretty much the same as everything.

00:10:03.840 --> 00:10:07.440
I mean, you'll need to make some research

00:10:07.440 --> 00:10:10.400
and get some people that will be vulnerable

00:10:10.400 --> 00:10:12.640
and that are quite hard to find.

00:10:13.280 --> 00:10:16.080
And for that, you need to maybe

00:10:17.119 --> 00:10:20.320
do a better job than others on recon.

00:10:20.880 --> 00:10:22.960
So yeah, that's the point, I guess.

00:10:23.679 --> 00:10:24.799
Yeah, here's a nice question.

00:10:24.799 --> 00:10:26.320
We'll do the roundtrip on this.

00:10:26.320 --> 00:10:28.159
From Pavel from Instagram,

00:10:28.159 --> 00:10:30.880
what is the best and the worst moment

00:10:30.880 --> 00:10:32.320
of your backbouncing career?

00:10:32.320 --> 00:10:33.840
Jason, what's the best moment

00:10:33.840 --> 00:10:35.119
of your backbouncing career?

00:10:38.559 --> 00:10:40.320
Best moment of my bugbouncing career,

00:10:40.320 --> 00:10:41.840
I think it was when I hit the number one

00:10:41.840 --> 00:10:42.799
on the Bug Crowd leaderboard.

00:10:43.840 --> 00:10:47.039
So very early on in Bug Crowd's history,

00:10:48.080 --> 00:10:51.599
in like the first 10 years of it being around,

00:10:53.280 --> 00:10:54.719
I was battling back and forth

00:10:54.719 --> 00:10:56.479
with another hunter named Bickwork

00:10:56.479 --> 00:10:58.320
to be number one on the leaderboard.

00:10:58.320 --> 00:11:00.320
And this was before bugbouncing

00:11:00.320 --> 00:11:01.679
was critical mass, right?

00:11:02.559 --> 00:11:04.159
So there was maybe, I would say,

00:11:05.440 --> 00:11:08.559
5,000 at most active hunters

00:11:08.559 --> 00:11:09.840
on the platform at that point.

00:11:09.840 --> 00:11:11.919
But it was still a really big deal for me.

00:11:12.640 --> 00:11:15.440
And so I won the number one spot

00:11:15.440 --> 00:11:19.760
and they did a little party at Defcon

00:11:19.760 --> 00:11:21.200
and they gave me a little badge

00:11:21.200 --> 00:11:22.080
and stuff like that.

00:11:22.080 --> 00:11:24.080
And so that was really special for me

00:11:24.080 --> 00:11:25.760
to hit that spot.

00:11:25.760 --> 00:11:26.799
I think that was cool.

00:11:26.799 --> 00:11:27.599
Yeah, nice.

00:11:27.599 --> 00:11:29.599
What is the best moment of your career?

00:11:29.599 --> 00:11:30.479
The best moment?

00:11:30.479 --> 00:11:30.979
Yes.

00:11:32.960 --> 00:11:34.559
That's a good question, to be honest.

00:11:35.280 --> 00:11:37.119
I guess that's when I started

00:11:37.119 --> 00:11:39.679
to get some very cool bugs.

00:11:40.320 --> 00:11:41.679
When I started on Swisscom,

00:11:42.799 --> 00:11:43.440
little too late,

00:11:43.440 --> 00:11:46.400
I learned the system,

00:11:46.960 --> 00:11:49.039
definitely much, much better.

00:11:49.039 --> 00:11:51.200
And I did started to get

00:11:51.200 --> 00:11:53.440
some very, very, very cool bugs.

00:11:53.440 --> 00:11:56.320
And they were like super cool with that.

00:11:56.960 --> 00:11:58.960
And they even were like impressed

00:11:59.039 --> 00:12:00.719
and that was definitely like

00:12:01.359 --> 00:12:04.000
really my first very cool bugs

00:12:04.000 --> 00:12:05.200
on Bug Monty.

00:12:05.200 --> 00:12:07.760
So yeah, that's a special feeling for that.

00:12:09.280 --> 00:12:10.400
Yeah, I guess that's...

00:12:10.400 --> 00:12:12.479
What is the worst moment of your career?

00:12:14.080 --> 00:12:15.440
I mean, I think it's always

00:12:15.440 --> 00:12:16.880
the worst moment when you have

00:12:16.880 --> 00:12:19.440
what you know is a really impactful bug.

00:12:19.440 --> 00:12:21.440
And like the client just doesn't get it

00:12:21.440 --> 00:12:22.400
and you submit it

00:12:22.400 --> 00:12:23.520
and they don't understand

00:12:23.520 --> 00:12:25.039
and you arbitrate for yourself

00:12:25.039 --> 00:12:25.840
and try to get people

00:12:25.840 --> 00:12:26.799
on the platform involved.

00:12:27.359 --> 00:12:28.559
And it just goes nowhere.

00:12:28.719 --> 00:12:30.239
And then you just have to abandon that bug.

00:12:30.239 --> 00:12:31.679
And I've had that several times

00:12:31.679 --> 00:12:33.760
and it feels really bad.

00:12:33.760 --> 00:12:35.359
Like it feels horrible.

00:12:35.359 --> 00:12:36.400
And there's just no recourse

00:12:36.400 --> 00:12:37.280
at some point, right?

00:12:37.280 --> 00:12:39.919
So those are some of my worst moments.

00:12:42.159 --> 00:12:43.599
Yeah, I think there's stuff like that.

00:12:43.599 --> 00:12:44.559
I mean, there's a lot of lows

00:12:44.559 --> 00:12:45.760
in bug bounty and self-doubt,

00:12:45.760 --> 00:12:47.679
but for the most part,

00:12:47.679 --> 00:12:48.479
it's a good experience

00:12:48.479 --> 00:12:50.239
if you're trying and succeeding.

00:12:50.239 --> 00:12:50.960
So yeah.

00:12:52.400 --> 00:12:54.159
I guess pretty much the same for me.

00:12:54.159 --> 00:12:57.840
I mean, like frustration is kicking out sometimes.

00:12:58.400 --> 00:13:00.239
Like you try to explain

00:13:00.239 --> 00:13:01.440
everything correctly

00:13:01.440 --> 00:13:03.119
and like you have the impression

00:13:03.119 --> 00:13:06.000
of never being heard sometimes.

00:13:06.799 --> 00:13:08.080
That's quite frustrating.

00:13:08.080 --> 00:13:10.400
But except from that,

00:13:10.400 --> 00:13:11.679
I think at the moment

00:13:11.679 --> 00:13:13.919
when I'm on a program

00:13:13.919 --> 00:13:15.599
and I decide to switch

00:13:15.599 --> 00:13:16.719
to another program,

00:13:16.719 --> 00:13:18.400
and I really have the impression

00:13:18.400 --> 00:13:20.239
that I will never find

00:13:20.880 --> 00:13:22.159
something as critical

00:13:22.159 --> 00:13:23.679
as what I already found.

00:13:24.400 --> 00:13:26.320
A bit of like the imposter syndrome,

00:13:26.880 --> 00:13:28.960
especially when I started,

00:13:29.760 --> 00:13:36.960
that was quite hard to just ignore it

00:13:36.960 --> 00:13:37.919
and just continue.

00:13:39.359 --> 00:13:40.239
That's also something

00:13:40.239 --> 00:13:41.520
that I'm trying to discuss

00:13:41.520 --> 00:13:42.880
with a lot of the community,

00:13:43.599 --> 00:13:44.479
with a lot of people

00:13:44.479 --> 00:13:46.000
from the community, excuse me.

00:13:46.000 --> 00:13:47.520
I'm trying to like explain

00:13:47.520 --> 00:13:49.599
that you should just ignore it.

00:13:49.599 --> 00:13:52.960
Just go, just go.

00:13:53.599 --> 00:13:55.520
Go hit the wall until it breaks.

00:13:55.679 --> 00:13:56.559
One of the things

00:13:56.559 --> 00:13:57.679
I talk about in the course

00:13:57.679 --> 00:13:58.960
is don't get emotionally

00:13:58.960 --> 00:13:59.760
attached to a bug.

00:14:00.799 --> 00:14:02.400
I know that people put

00:14:02.400 --> 00:14:03.359
a lot of their heart and souls

00:14:03.359 --> 00:14:04.799
into finding these things in time,

00:14:06.000 --> 00:14:06.799
but you can't

00:14:06.799 --> 00:14:07.520
because you just never know

00:14:07.520 --> 00:14:08.559
what the outcome is going to be

00:14:08.559 --> 00:14:09.679
to move on to the next thing.

00:14:10.559 --> 00:14:11.919
Yeah, it is so hard.

00:14:11.919 --> 00:14:12.799
Yeah, it is so hard.

00:14:14.000 --> 00:14:15.919
I mean, I'm still doing it.

00:14:17.119 --> 00:14:18.080
I know that's definitely

00:14:18.080 --> 00:14:18.960
not a good idea,

00:14:18.960 --> 00:14:21.599
but like you get like five bugs

00:14:21.599 --> 00:14:24.320
and you think that it will be like 100k

00:14:24.880 --> 00:14:26.320
and you can be like,

00:14:26.320 --> 00:14:28.320
oh, come on, just forget about it.

00:14:29.599 --> 00:14:31.039
But yeah, we all know

00:14:31.039 --> 00:14:33.520
that we should try to avoid that.

00:14:33.520 --> 00:14:35.679
But sometimes it's hard.

00:14:35.679 --> 00:14:36.880
Do you guys ever like,

00:14:36.880 --> 00:14:38.559
you report some good bugs

00:14:38.559 --> 00:14:39.440
and then the night

00:14:39.440 --> 00:14:40.799
that you report them or whatever,

00:14:40.799 --> 00:14:42.000
you're like you have trouble sleeping

00:14:42.000 --> 00:14:43.039
and you're like checking to see

00:14:43.039 --> 00:14:44.239
like if they triaged them.

00:14:44.239 --> 00:14:49.280
And you're like, yeah, definitely.

00:14:49.840 --> 00:14:51.280
That happened to me

00:14:51.280 --> 00:14:52.719
like one month ago

00:14:52.799 --> 00:14:54.960
with some crazy bugs.

00:14:54.960 --> 00:14:56.080
I did this with the LAC,

00:14:56.080 --> 00:14:58.239
but especially for us in Europe,

00:14:58.239 --> 00:14:59.280
I guess you can relate.

00:14:59.919 --> 00:15:01.280
But the triage often happens

00:15:01.280 --> 00:15:02.239
in the night hours.

00:15:02.239 --> 00:15:02.719
Yeah.

00:15:02.719 --> 00:15:04.479
So when I have like a really good bug

00:15:04.479 --> 00:15:05.520
and I'm really,

00:15:05.520 --> 00:15:06.880
I don't really want the response.

00:15:07.440 --> 00:15:08.960
You wake up in the night.

00:15:09.599 --> 00:15:10.880
I am looking at this email.

00:15:11.760 --> 00:15:12.400
To be honest,

00:15:13.039 --> 00:15:14.719
I say that I'm trying to like

00:15:15.440 --> 00:15:17.919
not be emotionally attached to my bugs,

00:15:17.919 --> 00:15:20.080
but I still have like a special inbox

00:15:20.080 --> 00:15:20.640
in my mail.

00:15:21.359 --> 00:15:23.440
And that's like the only notifications

00:15:23.440 --> 00:15:26.640
that can like wake me up during the night.

00:15:26.640 --> 00:15:28.799
I'm causing all notifications

00:15:28.799 --> 00:15:29.679
except those ones

00:15:29.679 --> 00:15:30.719
because I want,

00:15:30.719 --> 00:15:32.320
if I get a bounty,

00:15:33.039 --> 00:15:35.919
if I see that mail that say

00:15:36.719 --> 00:15:38.640
you earned some cash,

00:15:38.640 --> 00:15:40.640
it will definitely wake me up.

00:15:40.640 --> 00:15:41.599
How do you have it set up?

00:15:41.599 --> 00:15:43.200
Like what kind do you use

00:15:43.200 --> 00:15:45.200
on the notifications of this?

00:15:45.200 --> 00:15:47.119
I'm using like a custom one.

00:15:47.760 --> 00:15:48.719
Yeah, I have a custom one

00:15:48.719 --> 00:15:50.239
that just put everything

00:15:50.239 --> 00:15:51.119
filters

00:15:51.119 --> 00:15:53.359
and just throw me some notification

00:15:53.919 --> 00:15:56.400
with some nice effect.

00:15:56.400 --> 00:15:57.679
If it's on my computer,

00:15:57.679 --> 00:15:58.799
I have some nice effect

00:15:58.799 --> 00:16:00.799
with like dollars.

00:16:06.000 --> 00:16:07.280
It's like the HackerOne leaderboard

00:16:07.280 --> 00:16:08.320
when someone scores something.

00:16:08.320 --> 00:16:10.559
Yeah, pretty much the same.

00:16:10.559 --> 00:16:11.440
Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:16:11.440 --> 00:16:12.640
And the sound as well.

00:16:12.640 --> 00:16:13.359
Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:16:13.359 --> 00:16:15.119
Oh, this is me, this is me, this is me.

00:16:15.119 --> 00:16:16.719
Yeah, I have something,

00:16:16.719 --> 00:16:17.599
a free custom.

00:16:17.599 --> 00:16:18.239
Very cool.

00:16:18.239 --> 00:16:19.679
I learned through this as well.

00:16:20.000 --> 00:16:21.840
The worst moment of my career

00:16:21.840 --> 00:16:23.039
was during this event

00:16:23.039 --> 00:16:24.960
after like one and a half weeks

00:16:24.960 --> 00:16:25.919
into the virtual phase.

00:16:25.919 --> 00:16:26.880
I had no bugs.

00:16:26.880 --> 00:16:28.000
And it was my first event.

00:16:28.559 --> 00:16:29.599
And I was like, oh no,

00:16:29.599 --> 00:16:30.960
they will never invite me again.

00:16:30.960 --> 00:16:32.000
I feel so bad.

00:16:32.000 --> 00:16:33.599
The guys are so good.

00:16:34.479 --> 00:16:35.200
I knew nothing.

00:16:35.919 --> 00:16:37.520
And the best moment of my career

00:16:37.520 --> 00:16:38.719
was one week later

00:16:38.719 --> 00:16:40.239
when I actually found some bugs.

00:16:40.239 --> 00:16:40.880
Ah, there you go.

00:16:40.880 --> 00:16:42.080
And then I came here,

00:16:42.080 --> 00:16:43.599
I discussed with everyone

00:16:43.599 --> 00:16:45.200
and I learned so much.

00:16:45.200 --> 00:16:46.320
You know, yeah, yeah.

00:16:46.320 --> 00:16:47.599
So like my worst and best

00:16:47.599 --> 00:16:49.039
came one week apart.

00:16:49.039 --> 00:16:50.479
Yeah, that's definitely a good point

00:16:50.479 --> 00:16:52.880
because it happened to me also.

00:16:52.880 --> 00:16:53.200
Yeah.

00:16:53.200 --> 00:16:54.719
I'm going to a live hacking event

00:16:54.719 --> 00:16:55.359
with nothing.

00:16:56.000 --> 00:16:56.880
And I was like,

00:16:57.760 --> 00:16:59.679
damn, there's a lot of people

00:16:59.679 --> 00:17:00.799
finding a lot of things

00:17:00.799 --> 00:17:03.119
and I'm not finding anything.

00:17:03.119 --> 00:17:03.520
Yeah.

00:17:03.520 --> 00:17:05.839
And yeah, that's pretty hard to manage.

00:17:05.839 --> 00:17:07.199
But finally, I mean,

00:17:07.920 --> 00:17:09.040
we're quite successful.

00:17:09.040 --> 00:17:11.439
So sometimes it works,

00:17:11.439 --> 00:17:12.800
sometimes it just doesn't.

00:17:13.359 --> 00:17:14.479
Yeah, it's just,

00:17:14.479 --> 00:17:15.599
you have to keep at it.

00:17:15.599 --> 00:17:16.319
Yeah.

00:17:16.319 --> 00:17:17.280
It somehow works.

00:17:18.239 --> 00:17:21.199
OK, next question from David from Instagram.

00:17:21.199 --> 00:17:23.359
Is Backbone VFX progressing harder

00:17:23.359 --> 00:17:25.119
but easier as the years go by?

00:17:28.640 --> 00:17:30.079
Maybe start with you for a change.

00:17:30.079 --> 00:17:31.760
Yeah, for me,

00:17:31.760 --> 00:17:35.520
it's like just always the same things.

00:17:35.520 --> 00:17:37.119
We need to evolve

00:17:37.119 --> 00:17:38.640
with the new techniques

00:17:38.640 --> 00:17:39.839
and the new content

00:17:39.839 --> 00:17:41.439
and new technologies.

00:17:41.439 --> 00:17:43.439
And it's pretty much always the same.

00:17:44.400 --> 00:17:48.239
If you do like learning constantly,

00:17:48.239 --> 00:17:49.119
that's the point.

00:17:49.680 --> 00:17:52.079
It will be quite always the same, I guess.

00:17:52.959 --> 00:17:56.719
We saw a lot of more protective things.

00:17:56.719 --> 00:17:59.520
There are always some new technologies

00:17:59.520 --> 00:18:00.959
to protect things better.

00:18:00.959 --> 00:18:04.640
But we also found a lot of new techniques.

00:18:04.640 --> 00:18:07.040
And if you just stay constantly

00:18:08.400 --> 00:18:09.920
at a good technical level,

00:18:10.479 --> 00:18:14.479
I think it's quite like always the same thing,

00:18:15.199 --> 00:18:16.239
always the same level.

00:18:17.599 --> 00:18:19.680
I think it's hard.

00:18:19.680 --> 00:18:20.800
I don't want to lie to people, right?

00:18:20.800 --> 00:18:22.959
Like bug bounty is hard.

00:18:24.079 --> 00:18:24.880
It is much harder

00:18:24.880 --> 00:18:26.400
than what a lot of people come out

00:18:27.199 --> 00:18:28.319
ready to experience.

00:18:28.319 --> 00:18:30.400
So like I remember the transition

00:18:30.400 --> 00:18:33.680
from pen testing web apps full time as a job

00:18:33.680 --> 00:18:34.800
and then trying to do bug bounty

00:18:34.800 --> 00:18:35.680
and realizing like,

00:18:35.680 --> 00:18:37.760
oh, like a lot of the bugs

00:18:37.760 --> 00:18:39.040
that you find in pen tests

00:18:39.680 --> 00:18:41.280
are either not payable

00:18:41.280 --> 00:18:42.719
or you have to chain them

00:18:42.719 --> 00:18:44.959
so you get like way more impact.

00:18:45.680 --> 00:18:46.959
And then some of the bug classes

00:18:46.959 --> 00:18:48.640
are just not available on customers

00:18:48.640 --> 00:18:50.880
who have had a running bug bounty for a long time

00:18:50.880 --> 00:18:52.079
because they've already been remediated

00:18:52.079 --> 00:18:54.959
all that like kind of medium hanging fruit or whatever.

00:18:54.959 --> 00:18:56.479
And so it is harder.

00:18:56.479 --> 00:18:58.160
And so like if you expect to come out of,

00:18:59.520 --> 00:19:02.479
you know, like doing like a SANS course

00:19:02.479 --> 00:19:04.400
or like, you know, having like your CEH

00:19:04.400 --> 00:19:05.280
or something like that

00:19:05.280 --> 00:19:06.239
or some kind of training

00:19:06.239 --> 00:19:08.560
and just like kill it at bug bounties,

00:19:08.560 --> 00:19:10.079
it is a little bit of a rough awakening.

00:19:10.079 --> 00:19:12.000
It is much harder than that skill level.

00:19:12.000 --> 00:19:13.439
But once you put time into it,

00:19:13.439 --> 00:19:14.800
and I sincerely believe this,

00:19:15.680 --> 00:19:16.959
success in bug bounty

00:19:18.640 --> 00:19:20.079
is based on the time invested.

00:19:20.640 --> 00:19:22.880
Like once you become an expert

00:19:22.880 --> 00:19:24.880
of any of the applications or technologies

00:19:24.880 --> 00:19:27.359
that that program is asking you to look at,

00:19:27.359 --> 00:19:29.760
once you become like a power user of their app,

00:19:29.760 --> 00:19:30.880
like there are places

00:19:30.880 --> 00:19:32.719
where no other bug hunters will have tested.

00:19:32.719 --> 00:19:34.239
Eventually you'll find that

00:19:34.239 --> 00:19:35.439
and you'll find bugs

00:19:35.439 --> 00:19:36.400
and you'll find success,

00:19:36.400 --> 00:19:37.760
but it takes a lot of time.

00:19:37.920 --> 00:19:40.000
Yeah, I can 100% relate to this.

00:19:40.000 --> 00:19:41.280
I was a bandit and I was like,

00:19:41.280 --> 00:19:43.439
yeah, I know a lot of vulnerability classes.

00:19:43.439 --> 00:19:45.119
I'm studying bug bounty reports.

00:19:45.119 --> 00:19:46.079
I'm reading write-ups

00:19:46.079 --> 00:19:48.479
and I kind of know what they are finding also.

00:19:48.479 --> 00:19:49.280
Will it be easy?

00:19:49.280 --> 00:19:50.319
And then I was like,

00:19:50.319 --> 00:19:52.560
hmm, it is not as easy as I thought.

00:19:53.439 --> 00:19:54.959
Also I had to at the same time,

00:19:54.959 --> 00:19:56.400
like creating channels and stuff,

00:19:56.400 --> 00:19:59.119
like I had to learn all the business sides too.

00:19:59.119 --> 00:20:00.079
I was like, yeah, I can do,

00:20:00.079 --> 00:20:02.000
I can do both at the same time, not a problem.

00:20:02.000 --> 00:20:03.839
It turns out not so easy.

00:20:03.839 --> 00:20:07.119
So I had to like just focus more on bug bounties.

00:20:07.520 --> 00:20:10.239
Now it makes it work,

00:20:10.239 --> 00:20:13.680
but I was truly not expecting it to work.

00:20:13.680 --> 00:20:16.400
Bug bounty is definitely hard.

00:20:16.400 --> 00:20:18.640
The only point is people think

00:20:18.640 --> 00:20:21.760
that there's like a lot of other people

00:20:21.760 --> 00:20:22.640
that are killing it,

00:20:22.640 --> 00:20:24.079
but we are, in fact,

00:20:24.079 --> 00:20:25.760
we are not so much people

00:20:25.760 --> 00:20:28.800
that are really successful in bug bounty

00:20:28.800 --> 00:20:29.920
and that's not from the things

00:20:29.920 --> 00:20:31.599
that's because it's definitely hard.

00:20:33.199 --> 00:20:35.119
You also have the social media effect too, right?

00:20:35.119 --> 00:20:35.920
Oh yeah.

00:20:35.920 --> 00:20:39.119
Like you think that there's,

00:20:39.119 --> 00:20:40.959
on any given open program

00:20:40.959 --> 00:20:41.839
that's open to the internet,

00:20:41.839 --> 00:20:42.880
that's public,

00:20:42.880 --> 00:20:43.920
you think that there's,

00:20:43.920 --> 00:20:45.920
you know, thousands of people looking at it.

00:20:45.920 --> 00:20:46.880
I've been on the back end

00:20:46.880 --> 00:20:48.079
of a bug bounty platform

00:20:48.079 --> 00:20:49.199
and at any given moment,

00:20:49.199 --> 00:20:50.000
it's not thousands,

00:20:50.000 --> 00:20:51.520
it's more like, you know,

00:20:51.520 --> 00:20:53.280
50 or 60 at a time.

00:20:53.280 --> 00:20:55.520
So there is still opportunity there,

00:20:55.520 --> 00:20:57.439
but what you see on social media

00:20:57.439 --> 00:20:58.160
is everybody's saying,

00:20:58.160 --> 00:20:59.760
oh, I succeeded at this program.

00:20:59.760 --> 00:21:01.359
I've got 20K.

00:21:01.359 --> 00:21:02.400
And then you'll like get discouraged.

00:21:02.400 --> 00:21:02.719
You'll be like,

00:21:02.719 --> 00:21:03.839
I'm not even gonna hunt on that program.

00:21:03.839 --> 00:21:05.119
It's probably already taken care of.

00:21:05.199 --> 00:21:06.079
And it's like,

00:21:06.079 --> 00:21:07.680
that's actually not the truth most of the time.

00:21:07.680 --> 00:21:08.239
So yeah.

00:21:09.439 --> 00:21:10.880
Yeah, I have a question

00:21:10.880 --> 00:21:12.239
saying pretty much this.

00:21:12.880 --> 00:21:15.439
