00:30:31.199 --> 00:30:31.839
on backbouncing?

00:30:31.839 --> 00:30:34.079
I want to also do backbouncing,

00:30:34.079 --> 00:30:35.439
but I know that when I travel,

00:30:35.439 --> 00:30:36.880
it will be a little less.

00:30:36.880 --> 00:30:37.920
So it can be ideal,

00:30:37.920 --> 00:30:38.800
but obviously,

00:30:38.800 --> 00:30:41.280
with the flexibility of backbouncing,

00:30:41.280 --> 00:30:42.479
you can be anywhere,

00:30:42.479 --> 00:30:44.079
anytime, you have no meetings.

00:30:44.079 --> 00:30:46.959
So it's hard to find a job

00:30:46.959 --> 00:30:48.239
that's better suited

00:30:48.239 --> 00:30:49.599
for the digital nomad lifestyle.

00:30:50.239 --> 00:30:52.560
But you have to accept it.

00:30:52.560 --> 00:30:53.439
If you do it a lot,

00:30:54.719 --> 00:30:55.920
you have to balance it out,

00:30:55.920 --> 00:30:57.040
like how much do you work

00:30:58.400 --> 00:30:59.520
and things like this.

00:30:59.520 --> 00:31:00.560
For me at the moment,

00:31:00.560 --> 00:31:01.760
the priority is getting better

00:31:01.760 --> 00:31:02.560
at backbouncing.

00:31:03.280 --> 00:31:05.119
And being in Argentina

00:31:05.119 --> 00:31:06.800
for three months is super fun.

00:31:06.800 --> 00:31:09.520
But if I want to be laser focused,

00:31:09.520 --> 00:31:12.560
I'm better off just being at my home

00:31:12.560 --> 00:31:14.000
and not doing this.

00:31:14.000 --> 00:31:16.079
So it is a hard balance, it's fine.

00:31:17.280 --> 00:31:19.040
I'm useless without my home machine.

00:31:20.640 --> 00:31:21.920
I have like six monitors,

00:31:21.920 --> 00:31:23.040
and I know that's egregious, right?

00:31:23.599 --> 00:31:25.920
But if I don't have at least two monitors,

00:31:25.920 --> 00:31:27.439
one monitor that's primary burp

00:31:27.439 --> 00:31:29.599
and one monitor that's the application,

00:31:29.599 --> 00:31:30.560
I'm kind of useless.

00:31:32.560 --> 00:31:34.319
I went to the Apple,

00:31:34.319 --> 00:31:35.359
because in Poland,

00:31:35.359 --> 00:31:37.359
we don't have the Apple Visual Project

00:31:37.359 --> 00:31:37.839
to test it,

00:31:37.839 --> 00:31:39.119
because there's no Polish language

00:31:39.119 --> 00:31:40.160
in Syria and stuff like this.

00:31:40.160 --> 00:31:43.199
So I used my opportunity here to see it.

00:31:43.199 --> 00:31:45.199
And my main use case is not,

00:31:45.199 --> 00:31:46.079
they were showing me like,

00:31:46.959 --> 00:31:48.239
they were showing videos,

00:31:48.800 --> 00:31:51.199
different photos and stuff like that.

00:31:51.199 --> 00:31:53.119
Actually, what I'm interested in

00:31:53.119 --> 00:31:54.719
is to have the external screen

00:31:54.719 --> 00:31:56.479
with my mouse when I travel.

00:31:56.479 --> 00:31:58.239
And there wasn't much about it in the demo,

00:31:58.239 --> 00:32:01.280
but it would be cool if there is.

00:32:01.280 --> 00:32:02.959
I mean, that's a difficult point

00:32:02.959 --> 00:32:04.000
in my working event,

00:32:04.319 --> 00:32:05.839
that you just have your laptop

00:32:06.640 --> 00:32:07.760
with one screen.

00:32:08.560 --> 00:32:12.560
And definitely, it makes the productivity

00:32:13.599 --> 00:32:15.280
that's definitely a bit harder

00:32:15.280 --> 00:32:16.880
than being at home

00:32:16.880 --> 00:32:18.400
with my setup and everything.

00:32:18.400 --> 00:32:20.160
I love that HackerOne, though,

00:32:20.160 --> 00:32:21.599
has done it at that esports arena

00:32:21.599 --> 00:32:22.959
the last three years.

00:32:22.959 --> 00:32:24.319
And they have monitors there.

00:32:25.520 --> 00:32:27.439
If you get there early and get a seat,

00:32:27.439 --> 00:32:28.560
you can get an extra monitor.

00:32:29.680 --> 00:32:30.400
That's the goal.

00:32:30.400 --> 00:32:31.839
You need to get that early.

00:32:32.479 --> 00:32:36.640
So, it's definitely quite hard in Vegas, right?

00:32:36.640 --> 00:32:37.680
Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:32:37.680 --> 00:32:38.880
To be honest, I was surprised

00:32:38.880 --> 00:32:40.400
because in the life I go there,

00:32:40.400 --> 00:32:41.760
I barely saw anyone using

00:32:41.760 --> 00:32:43.199
these external screens.

00:32:43.199 --> 00:32:45.119
Oh, when I walked in on the second day,

00:32:45.119 --> 00:32:46.079
at least in the front part,

00:32:46.079 --> 00:32:46.959
I saw a ton of people

00:32:46.959 --> 00:32:48.160
using the external screens.

00:32:48.160 --> 00:32:49.119
Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:32:49.119 --> 00:32:50.400
People were sitting at the desk.

00:32:50.400 --> 00:32:52.000
It makes sense to use the screen.

00:32:53.760 --> 00:32:55.680
There was no HDMI cable.

00:32:57.520 --> 00:32:59.359
I had one, but a lot of people didn't.

00:32:59.359 --> 00:33:00.959
Yeah, that explains.

00:33:00.959 --> 00:33:02.239
That was the reason, I guess.

00:33:02.239 --> 00:33:02.739
Yeah.

00:33:03.520 --> 00:33:04.800
Okay, Jason, question to you.

00:33:05.599 --> 00:33:08.560
From DDRD, the future.

00:33:08.560 --> 00:33:10.479
Backbounty Driven is inconsistent.

00:33:11.040 --> 00:33:12.800
How to switch from Backbounty

00:33:12.800 --> 00:33:14.719
to Rectum or Penetration Testing

00:33:14.719 --> 00:33:15.839
and how to find clients?

00:33:16.800 --> 00:33:17.300
Yeah.

00:33:18.880 --> 00:33:21.280
So, I'm relatively lucky

00:33:21.280 --> 00:33:23.119
because I've spent the better part

00:33:23.119 --> 00:33:26.239
of my 20-year career in offensive security

00:33:27.280 --> 00:33:28.479
speaking and doing research

00:33:28.479 --> 00:33:29.520
and doing public talks

00:33:29.520 --> 00:33:30.560
and building a brand.

00:33:31.119 --> 00:33:33.920
And that's not a lot of people.

00:33:33.920 --> 00:33:35.520
A lot of people don't like to do that, right?

00:33:35.520 --> 00:33:36.560
It's a lot of extra work.

00:33:36.560 --> 00:33:37.199
It's a lot of writing.

00:33:37.199 --> 00:33:38.880
It's a lot of being on camera,

00:33:38.880 --> 00:33:41.520
answering questions, research time.

00:33:42.239 --> 00:33:44.800
And so, when I built the course,

00:33:44.800 --> 00:33:46.479
luckily, I had that brand

00:33:46.479 --> 00:33:48.079
and people came to it.

00:33:48.079 --> 00:33:50.640
And then, word of mouth from the course

00:33:50.640 --> 00:33:53.119
got around saying it was good, you know?

00:33:53.119 --> 00:33:56.160
And then, people started coming to me for work.

00:33:56.160 --> 00:33:57.680
They were like, hey, I took your class

00:33:57.680 --> 00:33:59.439
or I heard someone took your class.

00:33:59.439 --> 00:34:00.880
It was really awesome.

00:34:00.880 --> 00:34:03.439
We want you to do our pen test, our red team.

00:34:03.439 --> 00:34:04.640
We want to consult with you

00:34:04.640 --> 00:34:06.479
on like X, Y, and Z and stuff like that.

00:34:06.479 --> 00:34:09.280
So, it's really word of mouth referral

00:34:09.280 --> 00:34:11.679
for a lot of my work at my new consultancy.

00:34:13.280 --> 00:34:16.719
But, I mean, if you cut your teeth on Bugbounty,

00:34:16.719 --> 00:34:18.080
you'll certainly be able to do

00:34:18.080 --> 00:34:20.000
like most modern web app pen tests.

00:34:20.000 --> 00:34:22.080
So, the skill set, you know, graduation

00:34:22.080 --> 00:34:23.199
is actually not that hard.

00:34:24.320 --> 00:34:25.760
But, you know, other than that,

00:34:25.760 --> 00:34:27.600
I think that I'm a big believer

00:34:27.600 --> 00:34:29.040
that if you go out and put good things

00:34:29.040 --> 00:34:31.040
into the world, good things will come back to you.

00:34:31.040 --> 00:34:34.239
And so, you know, I go to a lot of conferences

00:34:34.239 --> 00:34:35.919
and speak on my experiences.

00:34:35.919 --> 00:34:38.159
I give away most of my methodology

00:34:38.159 --> 00:34:39.679
for free in talks a lot of times

00:34:39.679 --> 00:34:41.520
or in webinars or whatever.

00:34:41.520 --> 00:34:43.600
And I think that that goodwill comes back to me

00:34:43.600 --> 00:34:46.560
when, you know, someone who's much younger than me

00:34:46.560 --> 00:34:48.560
has gone through some of that content.

00:34:48.560 --> 00:34:49.919
They're like, oh yeah, I saw your stuff.

00:34:49.919 --> 00:34:51.840
And then, this year, we're looking at doing a pen test.

00:34:51.840 --> 00:34:53.760
Why don't you come do our pen test, you know?

00:34:53.760 --> 00:34:55.120
And it's like, I know that's not

00:34:55.120 --> 00:34:56.719
the traditional sales cycle,

00:34:56.719 --> 00:34:58.000
but that's just how it works for me.

00:34:58.719 --> 00:34:59.439
Yeah, yeah.

00:34:59.439 --> 00:35:05.040
I have a question to you from AMR on Twitter.

00:35:05.040 --> 00:35:06.560
Have you ever encountered a bag

00:35:06.560 --> 00:35:09.040
that you knew existed and just couldn't exploit it?

00:35:10.080 --> 00:35:11.520
Of course, I mean, that was...

00:35:14.719 --> 00:35:16.000
That's quite a goal.

00:35:16.000 --> 00:35:18.000
I have a lot of bags that I was sure

00:35:18.000 --> 00:35:20.479
it wasn't exploitable at all,

00:35:20.479 --> 00:35:24.159
but then I'm spending a lot of time on them.

00:35:24.159 --> 00:35:25.600
I have a very good example.

00:35:26.000 --> 00:35:29.280
I had a very small bag on an app,

00:35:29.919 --> 00:35:33.679
and I decided that maybe it was better,

00:35:33.679 --> 00:35:36.000
and I decided to spend a few months on it.

00:35:36.560 --> 00:35:37.360
And finally, I...

00:35:37.360 --> 00:35:38.560
A few months on one bag?

00:35:38.560 --> 00:35:39.060
Yeah.

00:35:39.840 --> 00:35:42.159
Finally, I did get remote code execution,

00:35:42.159 --> 00:35:44.159
and it was paid like 75k.

00:35:47.120 --> 00:35:49.280
But that would have been possible,

00:35:49.280 --> 00:35:51.120
that it was just nothing.

00:35:51.840 --> 00:35:53.439
So you always take the risk.

00:35:54.159 --> 00:35:56.800
But yeah, I mean, there's a lot of things

00:35:57.520 --> 00:36:01.199
that I never was able to exploit.

00:36:01.199 --> 00:36:03.840
And I even tried to share with some people

00:36:03.840 --> 00:36:05.760
that maybe I have a different skill set.

00:36:05.760 --> 00:36:10.080
That's also one of the strengths of the bag bounty,

00:36:10.080 --> 00:36:11.600
is you can share with people.

00:36:11.600 --> 00:36:13.280
We can try to collaborate.

00:36:13.280 --> 00:36:15.600
But sometimes it's just some dead end.

00:36:15.600 --> 00:36:16.719
So that happens.

00:36:16.719 --> 00:36:17.679
That happens a lot.

00:36:18.320 --> 00:36:20.159
And yeah, that happens.

00:36:20.159 --> 00:36:21.760
Definitely, definitely.

00:36:22.000 --> 00:36:26.000
I had one on a program where I was testing on it,

00:36:26.000 --> 00:36:27.199
and I found an asset,

00:36:27.199 --> 00:36:28.479
and I found SQL injection.

00:36:29.040 --> 00:36:33.040
And I went as far as to pull the table names

00:36:33.040 --> 00:36:35.919
and some like just cursory data out of the database.

00:36:36.479 --> 00:36:38.560
But at this company, they were very big.

00:36:39.120 --> 00:36:40.959
And so the people who ran the bug bounty

00:36:40.959 --> 00:36:42.080
were different than...

00:36:42.080 --> 00:36:44.239
It was a different team than the team who did

00:36:44.239 --> 00:36:46.159
kind of like patching of servers.

00:36:46.159 --> 00:36:49.679
And so the team that...

00:36:49.679 --> 00:36:50.800
I submitted the report,

00:36:50.800 --> 00:36:52.000
and I conclude all the information.

00:36:52.000 --> 00:36:54.000
The team that was the defensive team,

00:36:54.000 --> 00:36:55.679
whether it was DevSecOps or whatever,

00:36:55.679 --> 00:36:57.600
they fixed the bug because they saw the traffic.

00:36:58.159 --> 00:37:00.560
And they fixed the SQL injection.

00:37:00.560 --> 00:37:03.199
And then the triage team after that came in

00:37:03.199 --> 00:37:05.520
and was like, oh, we can't reproduce your bug.

00:37:05.520 --> 00:37:07.199
And I'm like, no, no, no, no.

00:37:08.399 --> 00:37:10.080
So I go back to look and it's fixed.

00:37:10.080 --> 00:37:12.399
And I'm like, oh, you guys must have fixed it.

00:37:12.399 --> 00:37:13.760
And they're like, we didn't do anything.

00:37:13.760 --> 00:37:15.679
The triages were like, we didn't do anything.

00:37:15.679 --> 00:37:17.520
And so I spent weeks talking with them.

00:37:17.520 --> 00:37:19.520
I have table names of your database.

00:37:19.520 --> 00:37:21.760
And they're like, yeah, there's no bug.

00:37:21.760 --> 00:37:22.560
We're not paying you.

00:37:22.560 --> 00:37:24.320
And so it was like super frustrating.

00:37:24.320 --> 00:37:26.639
And that was one I still remember to this day.

00:37:26.639 --> 00:37:29.120
I tried to arbitrate and just nothing happened.

00:37:29.120 --> 00:37:29.679
And I was like...

00:37:29.679 --> 00:37:30.320
Nothing happened.

00:37:30.320 --> 00:37:30.879
Nothing happened.

00:37:32.239 --> 00:37:35.600
That's part of the triage frustration from someone

00:37:35.600 --> 00:37:40.800
because the triage can always have a small discussion

00:37:40.800 --> 00:37:44.479
with the developers and try to get the information

00:37:44.479 --> 00:37:45.840
on how it was found.

00:37:45.840 --> 00:37:48.879
But not every companies are doing that.

00:37:49.600 --> 00:37:50.560
That's unfortunate.

00:37:51.280 --> 00:37:52.159
But it happens.

00:37:52.159 --> 00:37:55.199
And it's like super frustrating.

00:37:55.199 --> 00:37:56.719
It happens also to me.

00:37:57.280 --> 00:38:00.159
So yeah, that can be very frustrating.

00:38:00.159 --> 00:38:04.639
They recently started utilizing Justin Gardner's advice

00:38:04.639 --> 00:38:08.000
about recording the BMO, even for like simple bugs.

00:38:08.639 --> 00:38:12.000
One of the advantages is this, that if they fix the bug

00:38:12.000 --> 00:38:14.000
and it stops existing for some time,

00:38:14.000 --> 00:38:16.320
like you have the proof there inside,

00:38:17.280 --> 00:38:19.679
where it's harder to get into the situation.

00:38:19.679 --> 00:38:21.840
Sometimes you will not be able to get in there.

00:38:22.719 --> 00:38:24.159
I mean, like nowadays there's a whole bunch

00:38:24.159 --> 00:38:27.280
of free or really cheap screen recording apps

00:38:27.280 --> 00:38:28.879
like Loom and stuff like that.

00:38:28.879 --> 00:38:30.719
And you can just like do a quick video

00:38:30.719 --> 00:38:31.439
of like, here's the bug.

00:38:31.439 --> 00:38:32.959
Let me show you how to execute and be done.

00:38:32.959 --> 00:38:33.459
And that's it.

00:38:34.639 --> 00:38:36.239
I also did the voiceover often.

00:38:36.239 --> 00:38:37.040
And then I told them like,

00:38:37.040 --> 00:38:38.560
oh, here's the Victim's browser.

00:38:38.560 --> 00:38:39.919
Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:38:39.919 --> 00:38:42.239
It's like, I think it's nice.

00:38:42.239 --> 00:38:46.080
Yeah, but I mean, sometimes even that's not enough.

00:38:46.080 --> 00:38:48.560
Because I mean, they just can't reproduce it.

00:38:49.199 --> 00:38:51.040
And even if they have a video, they're like,

00:38:51.040 --> 00:38:52.879
yeah, okay, but it doesn't work there.

00:38:52.879 --> 00:38:53.840
So, yeah.

00:38:53.840 --> 00:38:55.439
Yeah, I mean, it's not, it's not.

00:38:55.439 --> 00:38:56.320
It's not better proof.

00:38:57.760 --> 00:38:59.199
But it, yeah, yeah, definitely.

00:39:00.399 --> 00:39:01.760
What do you think about the future

00:39:01.760 --> 00:39:02.959
of the bug bounty space,

00:39:02.959 --> 00:39:05.840
considering AI and the web-free industries?

00:39:08.479 --> 00:39:12.719
Yeah, so I did a talk last night

00:39:12.719 --> 00:39:14.639
called The Dark Side of Bug Bounty.

00:39:14.719 --> 00:39:16.080
And one of my slides was about

00:39:16.879 --> 00:39:20.159
how platforms are utilizing researcher attack data

00:39:21.199 --> 00:39:22.879
and training AI models with it

00:39:22.879 --> 00:39:24.959
to build their own vulnerability scanners

00:39:24.959 --> 00:39:26.320
and threat feeds that are going to go out

00:39:26.320 --> 00:39:27.679
as products to their customers.

00:39:28.320 --> 00:39:30.320
And I think without paying the bug bounty hunters

00:39:30.320 --> 00:39:32.560
for that research, I think it's very unethical

00:39:32.560 --> 00:39:34.239
to train on our data and our research

00:39:34.879 --> 00:39:36.399
and then just give it away.

00:39:36.399 --> 00:39:38.479
I think that a model like something like Detectify

00:39:38.479 --> 00:39:41.280
has when you build a vulnerability check for them.

00:39:41.280 --> 00:39:43.280
And then anytime they find it using their scanner,

00:39:43.280 --> 00:39:44.239
you get a kickback.

00:39:44.399 --> 00:39:46.639
I think that model is way more fair.

00:39:46.639 --> 00:39:48.479
But to just take it and run

00:39:48.479 --> 00:39:50.320
and start using it in different things,

00:39:50.320 --> 00:39:51.439
I think is pretty unethical.

00:39:52.719 --> 00:39:53.520
I agree with that.

00:39:54.159 --> 00:39:56.159
For me, AI is just a tool.

00:39:56.879 --> 00:39:58.159
I don't think that will,

00:39:58.159 --> 00:39:59.439
that will probably change

00:40:00.879 --> 00:40:03.120
the way we are doing things, maybe.

00:40:04.080 --> 00:40:07.679
I'm not sure that will change the industry per se.

00:40:07.679 --> 00:40:11.360
That will just be another tool in like a tool set.

00:40:11.919 --> 00:40:13.600
But I'm not sure that will change

00:40:14.399 --> 00:40:17.600
deeply how things are done

00:40:17.600 --> 00:40:22.320
and how the bug bounty industry will evolve.

00:40:22.959 --> 00:40:24.800
I mean, the next step for bug bounty

00:40:24.800 --> 00:40:28.639
is to target small companies

00:40:28.639 --> 00:40:31.600
and trying to get just bigger and bigger.

00:40:31.600 --> 00:40:34.080
That's quite a young industry.

00:40:34.080 --> 00:40:38.080
It has become maybe like 12 years ago, I guess.

00:40:39.199 --> 00:40:40.719
That's still a small industry

00:40:40.719 --> 00:40:42.399
and it just can't become bigger.

00:40:42.399 --> 00:40:44.000
When Jason started hunting,

00:40:44.000 --> 00:40:46.800
that's the bug bounty was created, wasn't it?

00:40:46.800 --> 00:40:47.300
Yep.

00:40:47.679 --> 00:40:50.800
And yeah, so I don't think that will change anything.

00:40:50.800 --> 00:40:53.040
That will just be another tool

00:40:53.040 --> 00:40:58.080
and there will be some other tools also later.

00:40:58.080 --> 00:41:00.320
And yeah, for me, that won't change anything.

00:41:01.120 --> 00:41:03.520
I think there's a lot of power right now

00:41:03.520 --> 00:41:05.760
with the current LLMs that exist

00:41:05.760 --> 00:41:09.439
to supercharge small portions of what we do.

00:41:10.000 --> 00:41:14.399
Like I use AI bots to build bypasses

00:41:14.399 --> 00:41:16.159
to very brittle regex checks

00:41:16.159 --> 00:41:17.600
for things like SQL injection,

00:41:17.600 --> 00:41:19.280
cross-site scripting, stuff like that.

00:41:19.280 --> 00:41:21.360
I use them to discover one-day vulnerabilities

00:41:21.360 --> 00:41:22.399
in the same vein, right?

00:41:22.399 --> 00:41:24.399
If some CVE has been published

00:41:24.399 --> 00:41:26.239
and that has an attack string.

00:41:26.239 --> 00:41:28.639
Like I know from my 20 years of experience

00:41:28.639 --> 00:41:30.959
that these are usually the ways to bypass

00:41:30.959 --> 00:41:33.679
very brittle regex filters for web applications.

00:41:33.679 --> 00:41:36.320
And so I know I put those into a bot

00:41:36.320 --> 00:41:39.040
and then the bot gives me a list of attack strings

00:41:39.040 --> 00:41:40.800
based on the CVE to try to bypass

00:41:40.800 --> 00:41:43.360
the original protection that they patch.

00:41:43.360 --> 00:41:45.120
So I'm using it in like small,

00:41:45.120 --> 00:41:46.399
little bite-sized things like that.

00:41:46.399 --> 00:41:49.600
I did a talk at Venn's Nahomcon

00:41:49.600 --> 00:41:50.879
on some of the stuff I do with it.

00:41:52.000 --> 00:41:54.639
What is the most complex you've discovered?

00:41:56.159 --> 00:41:56.879
The most what?

00:41:56.879 --> 00:41:58.479
The most complex.

00:41:58.479 --> 00:41:59.520
The most complex bug?

00:41:59.520 --> 00:42:00.020
Yes.

00:42:02.080 --> 00:42:03.040
Good question.

00:42:04.000 --> 00:42:06.000
I guess you have a lot of complex bugs.

00:42:06.399 --> 00:42:06.899
Yeah.

00:42:07.600 --> 00:42:10.399
Yeah, I have a lot of super complex bugs.

00:42:12.320 --> 00:42:13.919
I'd say in BagMonte it was

00:42:14.959 --> 00:42:18.719
the bug I spent like one month on it

00:42:18.719 --> 00:42:20.560
trying to get a chain.

00:42:21.439 --> 00:42:23.520
I guess that's my most complex code.

00:42:23.520 --> 00:42:29.439
It did go from blind SSRF to SSTI,

00:42:31.520 --> 00:42:35.679
five writes, two zero days involved in the thing

00:42:35.679 --> 00:42:36.959
and finally RCE.

00:42:36.959 --> 00:42:39.919
So yeah, definitely my most complex chain.

00:42:39.919 --> 00:42:41.760
And it was like maybe one month

00:42:42.639 --> 00:42:46.719
searching for it for eight hours a day

00:42:46.719 --> 00:42:48.080
for like one month.

00:42:48.639 --> 00:42:50.800
Definitely my most complex chain.

00:42:50.800 --> 00:42:52.719
Yeah, what did you do with the blind SSRF?

00:42:52.719 --> 00:42:54.560
Did you like find some internal domains?

00:42:55.760 --> 00:42:58.959
I found that in fact the blind SSRF

00:42:58.959 --> 00:43:04.719
was like some PDF, HTML2 PDF

00:43:05.280 --> 00:43:05.919
conversion.

00:43:06.479 --> 00:43:08.320
So I saw that finally it was like

00:43:08.879 --> 00:43:13.280
a sort of blind SSRF inside the renderer.

00:43:13.840 --> 00:43:17.439
And finally I was able to just

00:43:17.439 --> 00:43:19.199
take the context of the URL

00:43:19.919 --> 00:43:22.159
load some HTML file

00:43:22.159 --> 00:43:25.600
and it was like a specific PHP library

00:43:25.600 --> 00:43:27.520
that was like doing the conversion.

00:43:27.520 --> 00:43:28.879
So I found zero days on it

00:43:29.439 --> 00:43:32.159
and it was also using some other libraries.

00:43:32.159 --> 00:43:34.560
So I also found some zero days on it.

00:43:35.360 --> 00:43:39.040
And yeah, it was quite complex to be honest.

00:43:39.760 --> 00:43:41.280
And I was super proud

00:43:41.280 --> 00:43:43.760
when I did get RCE after like a month.

00:43:44.479 --> 00:43:46.320
And the program was also super cool

00:43:46.320 --> 00:43:47.439
because it just told me,

00:43:48.000 --> 00:43:50.479
yeah, just go for it to search.

00:43:50.479 --> 00:43:52.320
For now that's like not super important

00:43:52.320 --> 00:43:55.280
but if you think that you can get something better

00:43:55.280 --> 00:43:55.780
just do it.

00:43:56.719 --> 00:44:00.159
And the flow was there for like an entire month

00:44:00.159 --> 00:44:02.800
because I'm pretty sure they were like,

00:44:03.360 --> 00:44:06.879
okay, he said that maybe there is something more complex

00:44:07.600 --> 00:44:09.280
than something like more impactful

00:44:09.280 --> 00:44:11.199
but they were like not super.

00:44:12.719 --> 00:44:14.239
I'm not sure that they thought

00:44:14.239 --> 00:44:17.360
that I was able to get RCE at some point.

00:44:17.360 --> 00:44:20.959
I'm pretty sure they thought that won't happen

00:44:20.959 --> 00:44:22.479
but it definitely happened.

00:44:22.479 --> 00:44:24.560
So yeah, definitely my most complex thing.

00:44:24.560 --> 00:44:26.320
Yeah, nice, well done.

00:44:27.120 --> 00:44:31.679
I think mine were at a live hacking event

00:44:31.679 --> 00:44:32.959
and I had the opportunity to work

00:44:32.959 --> 00:44:35.199
with Shubs and Shanyo on a team.

00:44:36.000 --> 00:44:38.800
And I didn't do much on this team, honestly.

00:44:38.800 --> 00:44:41.840
Like my specialty was actually the post exploitation

00:44:41.840 --> 00:44:44.159
and just like I actually found the documentation

00:44:44.159 --> 00:44:45.040
for the code we were looking at

00:44:45.040 --> 00:44:48.879
but basically there was a Java app

00:44:48.879 --> 00:44:50.719
for the hospitality industry.

00:44:50.719 --> 00:44:53.840
And it basically managed everything that had to do

00:44:53.840 --> 00:44:56.879
with kind of certain hotels,

00:44:56.879 --> 00:44:58.159
doing check-ins, doing checkouts,

00:44:58.159 --> 00:44:59.520
handling credit cards, all this stuff.

00:45:00.159 --> 00:45:05.439
And a Shub's special is to go out in Docker Hub

00:45:05.439 --> 00:45:07.199
and find and see if any of these applications

00:45:07.199 --> 00:45:09.360
have been installed in a Dockerized container,

00:45:09.360 --> 00:45:11.679
pull it down and then turn a black box test

00:45:11.679 --> 00:45:13.600
into a source code audit.

00:45:13.600 --> 00:45:16.320
And so we spent I think three weeks

00:45:16.320 --> 00:45:18.159
tearing apart this Java app.

00:45:18.159 --> 00:45:20.800
And we got a CVE, we got like three CVEs for it.

00:45:20.800 --> 00:45:24.879
We found somewhere along the lines of 42 RCE

00:45:24.879 --> 00:45:28.239
in this app of different types of XML injection,

00:45:28.239 --> 00:45:30.719
XML entity injection, SQL injection,

00:45:30.719 --> 00:45:33.120
login bypass, all kinds of stuff.

00:45:33.120 --> 00:45:35.280
And then we used those bugs to pivot

00:45:35.280 --> 00:45:37.919
onto the internal of the network of the client

00:45:37.919 --> 00:45:40.879
and we could pretty much had full access to the client.

00:45:40.879 --> 00:45:44.719
So that was one of the funnest experiences.

00:45:44.719 --> 00:45:46.239
Again, I only played a small part in that.

00:45:46.239 --> 00:45:48.080
Shub's was the mastermind behind a lot of it.

00:45:48.080 --> 00:45:48.879
So, yeah.

00:45:48.879 --> 00:45:51.439
Well, yeah, probably you're doubting a little bit.

00:45:51.439 --> 00:45:51.939
Yeah, probably.

00:45:54.959 --> 00:45:58.080
How do you maximize the knowledge you just learn?

00:45:58.320 --> 00:46:01.120
Do you just try to look for the specific reliability

00:46:01.120 --> 00:46:01.919
in a bunch of programs?

00:46:01.919 --> 00:46:05.439
Do you maybe create some offline labs to introduce the bug?

00:46:05.439 --> 00:46:06.959
What do you tell me if you see a bug?

00:46:09.280 --> 00:46:12.080
To be honest, I'm creating a lot of internal labs

00:46:12.080 --> 00:46:14.239
for myself to test for things.

00:46:14.239 --> 00:46:15.760
But once I get the knowledge,

00:46:15.760 --> 00:46:20.239
I'm just going on some regular programs.

00:46:21.360 --> 00:46:25.280
If I have some more knowledge, I will just test more things.

00:46:25.280 --> 00:46:30.959
But that won't change how I test the apps.

00:46:32.080 --> 00:46:33.120
I get some more knowledge,

00:46:33.120 --> 00:46:35.280
so I'm testing just a bit more things.

00:46:36.000 --> 00:46:40.320
But yeah, for me, it doesn't change a lot of my methodology.

00:46:40.320 --> 00:46:44.000
But yeah, I have a lot of testing labs,

00:46:44.000 --> 00:46:48.399
testing environments locally to test some new patterns

00:46:48.399 --> 00:46:49.679
and some new things.

00:46:49.679 --> 00:46:50.879
Yeah, yeah, that's a good point.

00:46:50.879 --> 00:46:52.560
How much time does it usually take

00:46:52.639 --> 00:46:56.239
to create a test lab for a particular bug test?

00:46:56.239 --> 00:46:59.760
It really depends on what exactly.

00:46:59.760 --> 00:47:06.239
But I think my bigger labs are maybe two days of work

00:47:06.239 --> 00:47:10.879
to set up everything for some complex target

00:47:10.879 --> 00:47:12.159
or complex system.

00:47:12.159 --> 00:47:15.919
It can take up to maybe two days, something like that, yeah.

00:47:15.919 --> 00:47:16.419
Nice.

00:47:17.040 --> 00:47:17.679
How about you?

00:47:18.399 --> 00:47:20.959
I think I spend just as much time researching

00:47:20.959 --> 00:47:22.239
as I do hacking a lot of the time.

00:47:23.120 --> 00:47:25.760
We talked about it in my talk here at the Bug Bounty Village,

00:47:25.760 --> 00:47:30.000
but I am constantly, like you, collecting write-ups,

00:47:30.639 --> 00:47:32.959
parsing them for what was the attack string?

00:47:32.959 --> 00:47:34.800
What do I think the hacker mindset was?

00:47:34.800 --> 00:47:36.239
How did they find the function?

00:47:36.959 --> 00:47:38.399
Where was it in the application?

00:47:39.679 --> 00:47:42.159
What kind of impact did they have to prove with the bug?

00:47:42.159 --> 00:47:44.879
And I'm keeping it in notes or keeping it in my head,

00:47:44.879 --> 00:47:46.320
but mostly in notes.

00:47:46.320 --> 00:47:49.760
And then when I have 50 of those on a new bug class or something,

00:47:49.760 --> 00:47:52.639
I try to create a small methodology for myself to replicate.

00:47:53.919 --> 00:47:57.280
So yeah, like comprehensive notes, comprehensive research.

00:47:57.280 --> 00:47:59.199
It's not just bug bounty reports that I parse.

00:47:59.199 --> 00:48:01.520
I parse CVE details a lot.

00:48:01.520 --> 00:48:02.719
I parse blogs.

00:48:03.280 --> 00:48:05.919
I have a whole feed leaf full of researchers who I respect

00:48:05.919 --> 00:48:08.639
who they do a lot of hunting and they blog about it.

00:48:08.639 --> 00:48:15.040
And yeah, I'll try to turn it into a step-by-step methodology for myself.

00:48:15.040 --> 00:48:16.080
So yeah.

00:48:16.080 --> 00:48:18.560
Okay, last question, and we'll have it at the end.

00:48:19.280 --> 00:48:22.239
How do you deal with procrastination or nerve melt?

00:48:22.239 --> 00:48:25.120
And do you always have high levels of motivation with hunting?

00:48:28.399 --> 00:48:30.239
The laugh says it all.

00:48:31.840 --> 00:48:32.560
Yeah, no, I don't.

00:48:33.280 --> 00:48:34.959
I mean, it's a real big problem.

00:48:34.959 --> 00:48:36.479
It doesn't matter what you're doing, honestly.

00:48:37.120 --> 00:48:40.320
If you're a pen tester or whatever, or you're a bug bounty hunter.

00:48:41.600 --> 00:48:47.840
I think a lot of us have different sort of neurodivergence a little bit.

00:48:47.840 --> 00:48:49.520
I know a lot of testers with ADHD.

00:48:49.520 --> 00:48:54.159
I know a lot of testers who are on the autism spectrum.

00:48:54.159 --> 00:49:00.560
I know a lot of testers who just like, they're just procrastinators, right?

00:49:00.560 --> 00:49:02.080
And it's really hard.

00:49:02.080 --> 00:49:09.600
I think that I have a cascading set of alarms that helps me be productive.

00:49:09.600 --> 00:49:14.800
And then I didn't learn I had ADHD until I was 38.

00:49:14.800 --> 00:49:16.879
And so I was very late diagnosed.

00:49:16.879 --> 00:49:20.959
And now that I'm medicated a little bit, I can focus a little bit more on work during the day.

00:49:20.959 --> 00:49:23.919
But there's no easy answer.

00:49:23.919 --> 00:49:26.320
On the burnout phase too, it's like impossible.

00:49:26.320 --> 00:49:29.679
I'm sure you guys know, when you're on a roll, you don't want to stop.

00:49:29.679 --> 00:49:31.040
It doesn't matter what time it is, right?

00:49:31.040 --> 00:49:32.719
It doesn't matter if it's 3 a.m.

00:49:32.719 --> 00:49:35.600
If you're in the zone, you don't want to stop.

00:49:35.600 --> 00:49:38.639
And so if you're in the zone for a week and you're attacking this program,

00:49:39.760 --> 00:49:41.520
you can burn yourself out really easily.

00:49:42.159 --> 00:49:46.479
And then that burnout stacks with the triage issues that sometimes you have and stuff like that.

00:49:47.439 --> 00:49:51.439
Like we have this saying in the Discord that bug bounty is a scam.

00:49:51.439 --> 00:49:52.479
We just talk about it all the time.

00:49:52.479 --> 00:49:53.679
We're like, bug bounty is a scam.

00:49:53.679 --> 00:49:58.239
Because we just put all this effort into it and then just get a wreck or triage or something like that.

00:49:58.239 --> 00:49:59.199
And we get real angry.

00:49:59.199 --> 00:50:00.479
And we're like, it's a scam.

00:50:00.479 --> 00:50:01.600
We're not doing it anymore.

00:50:02.239 --> 00:50:03.120
Shut it all down.

00:50:03.120 --> 00:50:04.879
And then, of course, we feel better.

00:50:04.879 --> 00:50:05.520
Of course.

00:50:05.520 --> 00:50:07.600
Of course, you get a good bounty and you're like, oh, bug bounty is the best.

00:50:07.600 --> 00:50:08.159
Yeah, this is great.

00:50:08.159 --> 00:50:09.360
Bug bounty, yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:50:09.360 --> 00:50:09.919
Free money.

00:50:09.919 --> 00:50:10.959
Yeah, exactly.

00:50:12.080 --> 00:50:14.239
Yeah, to me, that's really a good question.

00:50:14.239 --> 00:50:20.399
Because at the start, I told you that bug bounty is giving you a lot of freedom.

00:50:21.120 --> 00:50:25.280
And that's also the freedom of procrastinating.

00:50:25.919 --> 00:50:28.399
And for me, it's quite important.

00:50:28.399 --> 00:50:31.840
I accepted that I will procrastinate at some time.

00:50:32.479 --> 00:50:33.919
That's something you need to accept.

00:50:33.919 --> 00:50:39.360
Because I like to value my mental health.

00:50:39.360 --> 00:50:41.280
And that's part of it.

00:50:41.280 --> 00:50:43.600
If I don't want to hack, I'm not hacking.

00:50:43.600 --> 00:50:48.639
That's why you also need to have a bit of money, to have a bit of savings.

00:50:48.639 --> 00:50:55.520
Because sometimes, if you rely only on yourself, that can be quite stressful.

00:50:55.520 --> 00:50:59.760
Because you want to procrastinate, but you will force yourself to just work because you

00:50:59.760 --> 00:51:00.879
need some money.

00:51:00.879 --> 00:51:05.600
And because of that, you will definitely go to the burnout very fast.

00:51:06.879 --> 00:51:10.000
The bug bounty is something that is quite complex.

00:51:10.000 --> 00:51:12.479
We have a lot of burnout and things like that.

00:51:13.360 --> 00:51:16.239
Lack of physical exercise, that doesn't help.

00:51:16.879 --> 00:51:21.439
And yeah, I guess it should be okay to just procrastinate.

00:51:21.439 --> 00:51:23.760
I'm a big video game fan.

00:51:23.760 --> 00:51:28.719
And sometimes, I even spend months on video games.

00:51:29.439 --> 00:51:30.159
What do you play?

00:51:30.959 --> 00:51:31.679
A lot of things.

00:51:32.399 --> 00:51:34.000
Right now, it's Elden Ring.

00:51:34.000 --> 00:51:35.040
Oh, such a good game.

00:51:35.040 --> 00:51:36.159
Yeah, yeah, definitely.

00:51:36.560 --> 00:51:37.060
Definitely.

00:51:38.639 --> 00:51:44.239
For example, this year, I decided that from January to May, I decided to just not work

00:51:44.239 --> 00:51:45.360
on bug bounty.

00:51:45.360 --> 00:51:46.560
I just didn't want to.

00:51:47.360 --> 00:51:51.199
I tried to build my French community a bit and things like that.

00:51:51.199 --> 00:51:57.199
And I decided to just play video games, take care of myself, do a bit of sports.

00:51:57.919 --> 00:52:00.639
And yeah, I mean, that's okay.

00:52:01.360 --> 00:52:08.560
But you need to have enough savings or things like that to accept the fact that

00:52:08.560 --> 00:52:10.239
sometimes, you're just not working.

00:52:11.439 --> 00:52:12.959
And that's definitely okay.

00:52:12.959 --> 00:52:16.159
That's part of getting some freedom from bug bounty.

00:52:16.159 --> 00:52:23.520
That's the good point for me from bug bounty, is getting the freedom to do whatever I want.

00:52:23.520 --> 00:52:28.320
And when I was Pentester, there's no way you can just say, okay, I'm not working.

00:52:29.040 --> 00:52:29.760
No, no, no.

00:52:29.760 --> 00:52:33.439
You're paid to work, so you just do your work.

00:52:34.239 --> 00:52:41.199
Yeah, that's a very good point of bug bounty for me, is being able to accept that I would

00:52:41.199 --> 00:52:44.399
just procrastinate and I'm just doing it.

00:52:44.399 --> 00:52:46.000
I'm not feeling guilty about that.

00:52:47.280 --> 00:52:51.679
I think I get the best of both worlds now, owning my own company, because I can say when

00:52:51.679 --> 00:52:52.639
and I don't work, right?

00:52:53.600 --> 00:52:55.439
But I can still do that Pentesting work.

00:52:56.320 --> 00:53:01.360
I feel like I've taken a couple of two or three weeks stints off for different things.

00:53:03.679 --> 00:53:07.360
But the gaming thing and taking time off, I mean, that's kudos to you, man.

00:53:08.399 --> 00:53:12.560
Knowing yourself, knowing when you see those signs of stress and burnout is really important.

00:53:12.560 --> 00:53:13.060
Yeah.

00:53:14.320 --> 00:53:19.679
After five months of gaming, jumping to the Ambassador Rewards company, absolutely killed.

00:53:21.280 --> 00:53:22.080
Yeah, definitely.

00:53:22.399 --> 00:53:23.439
Yeah, awesome.

00:53:24.479 --> 00:53:26.959
Thank you so much for awesome interviews.

00:53:26.959 --> 00:53:29.760
Guys, what are you looking forward to I see the rest of 20 years for?

00:53:31.360 --> 00:53:34.080
I mean, I'm looking forward to more classes.

00:53:34.080 --> 00:53:35.919
I really enjoy building the trainings.

00:53:35.919 --> 00:53:40.800
So we have more trainings coming out, not specifically for bug bounty hunters, but for

00:53:40.800 --> 00:53:43.439
red teamers and security leaders, too.

00:53:43.439 --> 00:53:47.600
So I've done a small stint as a CISO and I have very strong feelings about what a modern

00:53:47.600 --> 00:53:49.199
security leadership position should look like.

00:53:49.199 --> 00:53:52.719
So I'm going to build some classes around those things and I'm really excited to see

00:53:52.719 --> 00:53:53.280
where those go.

00:53:54.080 --> 00:53:54.580
Yeah.

00:53:55.199 --> 00:53:56.239
For me, it's great, isn't it?

00:53:56.239 --> 00:54:01.760
I mean, I will continue to help the community and we'll win the World Cup, obviously.

00:54:01.760 --> 00:54:02.479
So yeah.

00:54:02.479 --> 00:54:04.000
Hopefully second place behind Pro League.

00:54:05.360 --> 00:54:06.000
Maybe, maybe.

00:54:08.159 --> 00:54:12.320
I try to not brag too much because generally that can be bad for me.

00:54:12.320 --> 00:54:19.600
But yeah, I'm pretty, pretty sure that we'll do, at least we'll do some good, good plays,

00:54:19.600 --> 00:54:20.100
I guess.

00:54:20.639 --> 00:54:24.479
So yeah, I focus on that, doing some bug bounty.

00:54:24.479 --> 00:54:29.360
I just started to get back to bug bounty like a couple of months ago.

00:54:29.360 --> 00:54:34.320
So yeah, the objective is to get some good bounties for this year.

00:54:34.320 --> 00:54:36.959
So yeah, it's a goal.

00:54:36.959 --> 00:54:37.439
Yeah.

00:54:37.439 --> 00:54:38.719
Thank you guys so much.

00:54:38.719 --> 00:54:39.199
Thank you.

00:54:39.199 --> 00:54:40.159
Thank you for having us.

00:54:40.159 --> 00:54:41.840
Thanks a lot.

